---
import { mastheadStamp } from "../../dossiers/headline.js";
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import EveryCut from '../../components/EveryCut.astro';
import GuideData from '../../components/GuideData.astro';
import {bySlug,kicker} from '../../dossiers/registry.js';
import {schoolModel} from '../../dossiers/schools.model.js';
import {schoolChart,schoolNumbers} from '../../dossiers/schools.charts.js';
const D=bySlug.schools;
const model=schoolModel();
const groups=[...new Set(model.plates.map(p=>p.group))];
const period=model.plates.find(p=>p.bundle==='schools-national').period;
const cmsePeriod=model.plates.find(p=>p.id==='bill-items').period;
const N=schoolNumbers(model);
---
<Base title="Schools · Measure of India" description={D.description} nav="guides" dateStamp={mastheadStamp(D)} crumbLeft={`<a href="/guides/">Guides</a> / Education / <b>Schools</b>`} crumbRight={`${D.statusNote}, <b>${D.version}</b>`} canonical="/guides/schools/" ogImage="/og/schools.png">
<div class="page">
 <div class="titleblock break" data-inputs="schools-national, schools-cmse-33" data-prose="evergreen and data-bound facts">
  <div class="kicker">{kicker(D)}</div>
  <div class="hero-grid"><h1 set:html={D.headline}/><p class="lede">Almost every Indian child now starts school. Far fewer finish it. This guide follows children from pre-primary to class 12, looks at the schools and teachers they find there, and counts what families pay along the way.</p></div>
  <div class="meta"><span>School census <b>{period}</b></span><span>Household survey <b>{cmsePeriod}</b></span><span>Sources <b>UDISE+ and the NSS education survey</b></span></div>
  <div class="numbers">
   <div class="num"><div class="l">Pupils enrolled, pre-primary to class 12</div><div class="v acc" id="n-enrolment">{N["n-enrolment"]}</div><div class="d">Recognised schools, {period}</div></div>
   <div class="num"><div class="l">Schools</div><div class="v" id="n-schools">{N["n-schools"]}</div><div class="d">Recognised schools, {period}</div></div>
   <div class="num"><div class="l">Teachers</div><div class="v" id="n-teachers">{N["n-teachers"]}</div><div class="d">All school types, {period}</div></div>
   <div class="num"><div class="l">A family's school spending per child</div><div class="v" id="n-school_bill">{N["n-school_bill"]}</div><div class="d">Average for the academic year, coaching apart</div></div>
  </div>
 </div>
 <section data-inputs="schools-national, schools-cmse-31, schools-cmse-33" data-prose="evergreen"><div class="sec-head"><h2>What it takes to keep a child in school</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="reading-cols"><div class="reading-col"><p>Getting a name onto a school register is the easy part. A child also needs a teacher who turns up, a toilet that works, a way to get there and a family that can keep paying for books, uniforms and the bus. A girl in class 9 in a village has to clear each of these every year to reach class 12.</p><p>Most children in villages study in government schools, where fees are low or nil. In towns, more than half go to private schools, and the family bill is many times higher. On top of the school bill, many families pay for private tuition.</p></div><div class="reading-col"><p>Two sources tell the story. UDISE+, the Ministry of Education's census of schools, counts every recognised school, its pupils, teachers and facilities. A household survey by the National Statistical Office asked families in April to June 2025 where their children study and what they spend.</p><p>Neither measures learning. A child on the register may not attend every day, and a child who attends may not be able to read. Learning surveys are a separate subject, outside this guide.</p></div></div>
 </section>
 <section data-inputs="schools-udise-institution-2, schools-udise-student-2, schools-national, schools-cmse-33" data-prose="methodology"><div class="sec-head"><h2>How to read these numbers</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="wm-grid"><div><p><b>Enrolment ratios.</b> The gross enrolment ratio compares the pupils at a stage with the children of the official age for it. Pupils who are older or younger also count, so the ratio can pass 100 even when some children of the right age are out of school. In {period} it was <b id="n-primary_ger">{N["n-primary_ger"]}</b> at primary, <b id="n-secondary_ger">{N["n-secondary_ger"]}</b> at secondary and <b id="n-higher_ger">{N["n-higher_ger"]}</b> at higher secondary.</p><p><b>Leaving and staying.</b> The dropout rate is the share of pupils at a stage who are not enrolled the next year: <b id="n-dropout">{N["n-dropout"]}</b> at secondary. Retention looks over a whole cycle: of children who started class 1, <b id="n-retention_12">{N["n-retention_12"]}</b> reach class 12. Both are worked out from enrolment counts, not by following children one by one.</p><p><b>A change in how pupils are counted.</b> Until 2021-22 schools reported their totals. Since 2022-23 every pupil is a separate record. Figures on either side of that change are kept apart and are not read as one trend.</p><p><b>Two sources, two populations.</b> The school census covers recognised schools. The household survey covers children in surveyed households and their spending; its averages are per enrolled child, and private coaching is counted separately.</p></div><div class="wm-rail"><aside class="margin-note"><span class="mk">States differ in size</span>A small union territory and a large state each give one figure. A state's ratio can be distorted when children cross its border to study.</aside><aside class="margin-note"><span class="mk">Some ratios are withheld</span>Where a group's child population is tiny, UDISE+ prints enrolment ratios in the thousands of per cent. Those cells are left blank here.</aside></div></div>
 </section>
 <section id="current-reading" data-inputs="schools-national, schools-cmse-31, schools-cmse-38, schools-cmse-40" data-prose="data-bound facts"><div class="sec-head"><h2>What the numbers show</h2><span class="line"></span><span class="unit">{period}</span></div>
  <div class="reading-cols"><div class="reading-col"><p>The biggest leak is after class 10. Only <b id="n-transition_11">{N["n-transition_11"]}</b> of pupils who finish class 10 move on to class 11 the next year, and higher-secondary classes have the most pupils per teacher, <b id="n-ptr_hs">{N["n-ptr_hs"]}</b>.</p><p>Government runs <b id="n-govt_school_pct">{N["n-govt_school_pct"]}</b> of schools but teaches <b id="n-govt_pupil_pct">{N["n-govt_pupil_pct"]}</b> of pupils: its schools are many and small, often in villages, while private schools are fewer and larger.</p></div><div class="reading-col"><p>A year in a government school cost a family <b id="n-govt_bill">{N["n-govt_bill"]}</b> on average; a year in a private unaided school, <b id="n-private_bill">{N["n-private_bill"]}</b>. Course fees are the largest single item.</p><p>Private coaching is now a second school system. <b id="n-coaching_pct">{N["n-coaching_pct"]}</b> of pupils were taking it during the year, and a higher-secondary pupil who took it spent <b id="n-coaching_bill_hs">{N["n-coaching_bill_hs"]}</b> on it.</p></div></div>
 </section>
 {groups.map(group=><section data-inputs={[...new Set(model.plates.filter(p=>p.group===group).map(p=>p.bundle))].join(', ')} data-prose="evergreen explanations and data-bound values"><div class="sec-head"><h2>{group}</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  {model.plates.filter(p=>p.group===group).map(p=><div class="break"><figure id={'cb-'+p.id} data-inputs={p.bundle}>
   <div class="plate-head"><div class="t"><h3>{p.title}</h3><div class="sub">{p.sub}</div></div><div class="plate-val"><div class="v" id={'pv-'+p.id}>{N['pv-'+p.id]}</div><div class="c">{p.rows[0].name}</div></div></div>
   <div class="frame"><div class="plotzone" id={'plot-'+p.id} set:html={schoolChart(p,model)} /><div class="plotfoot"><span class="src">{p.bundle.includes('cmse')?'NSS household education survey, April to June 2025':'Ministry of Education, UDISE+, '+p.period}.</span><span class="dl"><button data-dl="svg">SVG</button><button data-dl="png">PNG</button><button data-dl="csv">CSV</button><a href={p.bundle.includes('cmse')?'https://www.mospi.gov.in/':'https://dashboard.udiseplus.gov.in/report2025/'} target="_blank" rel="noopener">Source ↗</a></span></div></div>
   <div class="explain"><div class="ev"><span class="mk">How to read this</span><p>{p.explain}</p></div></div>
  </figure></div>)}
 </section>)}
 <section data-inputs="schools-national, schools-udise-student-31, schools-cmse-33, schools-cmse-40" data-prose="evergreen"><div class="sec-head"><h2>Common misreadings</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="reading-cols"><div class="reading-col"><p><b>"Enrolled means attending."</b> A name on the register says nothing about how many days a child is in class, or what the child learns there.</p><p><b>"A pupil-teacher ratio is class size."</b> It is an average over the whole country. Single-teacher schools and crowded classrooms sit inside the same average.</p></div><div class="reading-col"><p><b>"Enrolment fell sharply after 2021."</b> The count changed from school totals to individual pupil records in 2022-23, which removed duplicate entries. The two periods measure differently.</p><p><b>"The average school bill is what most families pay."</b> Averages are pulled up by a minority of expensive private schools. Families with children in government schools typically pay far less.</p></div></div>
 </section>
 <EveryCut slug="schools"/><GuideData slug="schools"/>
 <section data-inputs="schools-national, all schools-udise and schools-cmse bundles" data-prose="methodology"><div class="sec-head"><h2>Sources and method</h2><span class="line"></span></div><p>School counts, enrolment, teachers, ratios and facilities come from the Ministry of Education's UDISE+ system: the national figures from its report for {period}, the state figures and history from the tables MoSPI publishes on its eSankhyiki portal. Spending and coaching come from the NSS Comprehensive Modular Survey on education, April to June 2025. The charts above answer one question each; every table, state, gender and school-type cut is in the table above and the downloads.</p><p><a href="https://dashboard.udiseplus.gov.in/report2025/">UDISE+ reports</a>. <a href="https://www.mospi.gov.in/">MoSPI</a>. Data revision date: <b id="prov-vintage">{model.plates[0].vintageText}</b>; neither source publishes one.</p></section>
</div>
<script>import '../../dossiers/guide.client.js';</script>
</Base>
